WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.420
Today, I'm going to talk about
testing with an expert, Keith

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.369
of Keith's Speaking Academy.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.510
We're going to talk about IELTS,
TOEFL, TOEIC, how these different

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.770
tests differ and how they are
important and how we prepare for them.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.970
So without further ado,
let's hear from Keith.

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:20.470
So today, you know, the subject
I want to get into is testing

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:25.430
because testing is such a big part
of, especially English learning.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.959
Nowadays, all languages, they have
their test ... whether it be Chinese,

00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:35.210
Korean, they all have their standard
test, you see, and, but in the

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.689
case of English, there are a number
of sort of authoritative tests.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:44.719
So my first question is, what are
the main recognized and unrecognized?

00:00:45.179 --> 00:00:48.839
Tests of English, whether it be for
academic purposes, for employment

00:00:48.849 --> 00:00:53.309
purposes, for immigration purposes,
what are the main tests and, and,

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:57.779
uh, what is their sort of respective,
you know, role or importance?

00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:00.919
If we're talking about tests, you're
mentioning they're like, I think,

00:01:00.919 --> 00:01:05.279
proficiency tests, which are tests to
gauge the level of a person in language,

00:01:05.289 --> 00:01:06.550
how well they can use the language.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:10.809
So the big tests, I mean, IELTS
is one of the biggest tests.

00:01:10.809 --> 00:01:15.130
It's, it's by Cambridge, it's by a
tri-partner, there's Cambridge, there's

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.219
IDP, and there's British Council, but
it's created by Cambridge assessment.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:22.239
So IELTS is, is one of the larger...

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.339
and what does IELTS stand for?

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:28.179
It stands for the International
English Language Testing System.

00:01:29.219 --> 00:01:29.799
Okay.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:30.649
So it's a test...

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:34.139
so if I hear someone say the
Cambridge test, he's actually,

00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:35.500
or she is talking about

00:01:35.669 --> 00:01:36.300
IELTS.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.195
IELTS is one of the Cambridge tests.

00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:45.664
So Cambridge have a suite of tests, um,
which include, um, IELTS, but it also

00:01:45.664 --> 00:01:50.214
includes their tests for young learners,
kind of movers, starters, and flyers.

00:01:50.585 --> 00:01:54.875
They have tests for school children,
moving up the level, going up to

00:01:55.004 --> 00:02:00.084
KET, PET, um, and they've got what
first, I think they now call it

00:02:00.114 --> 00:02:02.214
B2 first, advanced proficiency.

00:02:02.794 --> 00:02:06.854
So they have different, I guess they'd
be more achievement tests that they're

00:02:06.854 --> 00:02:10.545
actually measuring your ability at
a particular level, and they're all

00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:16.215
correlated to the CEFR, which is the
Common European Framework of Reference,

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.115
which you're probably familiar with.

00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:19.955
And so that.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.030
Yeah, so Cambridge are probably one of
the biggest organizations that do that.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.850
You know, that said, I mean,
you've also got Pearson.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.880
They're another testing organization,
the Pearson Test of English.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.920
And there are other organizations that
do testing at school level, at A levels,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.650
and baccalaureate level, internationally.

00:02:37.799 --> 00:02:39.849
So there's a whole bunch
of them, actually, a lot.

00:02:40.169 --> 00:02:44.279
Okay, now if we're talking about
adults, I know that certainly my

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:49.190
Asian experience has been that
TOEIC and TOEFL seem to be...

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.400
Particularly important.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.200
Like more people take those tests.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.620
That's why I say I don't know
who takes the IELTS and who

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.290
takes TOEIC and who takes TOEFL.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.000
Can you compare those three?

00:02:58.009 --> 00:02:58.559
Sure.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.680
I mean, TOEIC tends to be more
of a business kind of test.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.529
So people who are going into
a business place, possibly the

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.349
workplace, would take TOEIC.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.279
TOEFL is similar to IELTS, but
IELTS claims to be, and probably

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:13.730
is a more communicative test.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.849
TOEFL is more traditionally more
popular for going to America.

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:22.459
So if you're migrating or studying in
America, um, TOEFL would be the one if

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:26.320
you're going to North America, that,
that would be the, the, the test to take.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.000
Um, so a lot of countries in Asia
have traditionally taken TOEFL.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.029
However, in the last 10, 20 years iELTS,
which many people associate with Britain,

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:38.260
has been going to British universities.

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:43.169
Actually, IELTS is recognized by
hundreds of thousands of American

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:46.390
universities, North American
universities, as well as around the world.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.549
So nowadays, that difference between
should I do TOEFL or IELTS is a common

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:56.049
question a student in China may have,
because actually they could do either.

00:03:56.665 --> 00:04:01.615
Um, they just need to make sure that
it is recognized by the particular

00:04:01.635 --> 00:04:03.215
institute that they want to go to.

00:04:03.265 --> 00:04:08.435
If we put TOEIC aside as being more of
a, you know, employment related test,

00:04:08.455 --> 00:04:10.785
but the actual substance of the tests...

00:04:10.974 --> 00:04:18.855
they are  similar, except that IELTS
has a face to face spoken module.

00:04:18.985 --> 00:04:21.874
So both tests will test all
of the four skills, reading,

00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:23.115
writing, listening, and speaking.

00:04:23.374 --> 00:04:27.764
At the moment, I'm not sure exactly
what the speaking test is with TOEFL.

00:04:27.964 --> 00:04:31.845
If my memory serves me rightly, it used
to be like a recording, where you would

00:04:31.845 --> 00:04:37.869
record your voice, and then somebody
would assess that but the IELTS test,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.219
because they put such huge importance
on it being a communicative test,

00:04:41.599 --> 00:04:45.779
having that face to face interaction
with an examiner, where they ask you

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.289
questions and you answer, that's one
of the key differences for IELTS.

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:51.839
That is the ultimate test, isn't it?

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:57.379
I mean, I know a lot of people can train
on certain tests and get quite good at

00:04:57.379 --> 00:05:02.219
answering multiple choice questions, but
when you're face to face in an interview,

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.119
that is the ultimate test, I think.

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:04.739
I think it is.

00:05:04.739 --> 00:05:06.919
I think that's why it's
so widely recognized.

00:05:06.979 --> 00:05:12.025
And I think that's why employees,
international professional bodies like

00:05:12.025 --> 00:05:16.559
the ACCA for accountants recognize it,
like immigration boarders recognize it,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.345
and universities, of course, because
there's a higher level of, not security,

00:05:21.345 --> 00:05:25.704
but knowing that what the student
is saying they can do, they probably

00:05:25.735 --> 00:05:27.125
can because they've got that score.

00:05:27.164 --> 00:05:30.765
But it must make it more expensive
because now you have to have examiners

00:05:30.765 --> 00:05:34.434
that are devoting that much time
to interviewing each test taker.

00:05:34.554 --> 00:05:35.395
It probably does.

00:05:35.395 --> 00:05:38.964
I don't know the price comparison,
but certainly, yes, it's a higher

00:05:39.034 --> 00:05:41.485
cost if you've got that face
to face examiner doing that.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:45.640
So, yeah, and it makes it
logistically a lot more complicated.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.260
I mean, one of the keys to many
tests is keep it simple, right?

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.490
There's a balance between getting
the information you need, but

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.679
keeping it simple so that you
don't overload the student.

00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:00.049
And from the administration side, you want
a test that's fairly easy to administer.

00:06:00.699 --> 00:06:04.199
IELTS kind of kicks that
aside and says, we, we want.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:08.250
The good quality communicative tests,
we're willing to invest in teachers,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.620
examiners, having a face to face test.

00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:14.510
Um, although there is now in some
countries, there's an online element ever

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:18.510
since we had the pandemic, the, the idea
of having a face to face interview like

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.289
you and me now, it can be done on online.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.539
Right, right.

00:06:22.539 --> 00:06:23.980
But it's still the examiner's time.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.340
It's exactly, exactly, that's the thing.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:32.169
So, in terms of preparing for these tests,
are there good ways to prepare, and is

00:06:32.169 --> 00:06:35.869
it different depending on whether you're
preparing for IELTS, TOEIC, or TOEFL?

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.810
It's a great question, because
the classic way you prepare for a

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:42.079
test, like when you were at school,
you had the end of term test.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.329
You know the content
that's going to be tested.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.849
And so you would prepare
that content, right?

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:51.929
But for the proficiency test, like
IELTS, you actually don't know the

00:06:51.939 --> 00:06:56.159
content because it's not measuring
if you've learnt a specific course.

00:06:56.179 --> 00:06:57.990
It's wide, it's wide open.

00:06:58.479 --> 00:06:59.959
So the preparation has to be different.

00:07:00.039 --> 00:07:02.789
I guess you have to be clear
about the score you need.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.470
So for some people, a
band 5 will be enough.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.610
For other people, a band 7 will
be necessary, so they'll have to

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.069
invest more time in preparing.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.579
I mean, my strong belief, and I, and
I say this because I'm familiar with

00:07:16.589 --> 00:07:20.169
how the test is constructed and made,
is that the best way to prepare is

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:22.819
to develop your communicative skills.

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:27.049
So your ability to speak and communicate,
your ability to write in English.

00:07:27.485 --> 00:07:29.454
Your ability to read and to listen.

00:07:29.655 --> 00:07:32.885
So I encourage students actually to
spend 80 percent of their time developing

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.365
those skills like they would anyway.

00:07:35.905 --> 00:07:38.384
And then 20 percent is
around exam technique.

00:07:38.804 --> 00:07:42.884
So you do need to be familiar
with the question type, the exam

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:44.244
technique that you're going to need.

00:07:44.484 --> 00:07:46.645
Timing, it catches many people out.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:48.204
Oh, I didn't know I only had half an hour.

00:07:48.204 --> 00:07:54.789
You need to know how it works, question
types, and techniques to make sure you

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:56.579
get the score that you're trying to get.

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.679
So people can find examples of
previous tests for these tests?

00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:04.749
Yes, for TOEFL, for IELTS, TOEIC,
there are previous tests, what they

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:09.709
call practice tests, on most of those
websites, IELTS website, TOEFL website,

00:08:10.099 --> 00:08:14.610
and they'll have practice tests that
students can go in, they can see

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.169
the format, they can practice them.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:21.580
A lot of these organisations now
have free videos, courses, even mini

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.740
courses to guide you through and say,
listen, this is the question type.

00:08:24.969 --> 00:08:26.379
This is how you should be answering.

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:30.840
What they don't reveal, ever, is the
actual question you're going to be asked.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:31.390
Right.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:33.079
That's like letting
the cat out of the bag.

00:08:33.179 --> 00:08:33.770
Right.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.829
But the question could be,
uh, on any subject matter.

00:08:37.829 --> 00:08:39.799
It could be on, uh, global warming.

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:43.200
It could be on, uh, I don't know
about nuclear, nuclear physics,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.030
but like it could be on anything.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:49.750
And so what I hear you saying is
that essentially people need to spend

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:55.234
the bulk of their time um, Basically
upgrading their overall language skills,

00:08:55.264 --> 00:08:59.704
vocabulary, listening, comprehension,
reading comprehension, reading speed,

00:08:59.774 --> 00:09:05.794
ability to speak, ability to write,
accuracy of usage, all of these things.

00:09:05.844 --> 00:09:10.925
And then just spend maybe that remaining
15, 20 percent of their time trying to,

00:09:10.964 --> 00:09:12.704
you know, ace the system, so to speak.

00:09:13.604 --> 00:09:14.230
Ace the system.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:15.109
Yes, so to speak.

00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:15.619
Exactly.

00:09:15.829 --> 00:09:18.730
Yeah, learning the technique they
need to get the score that they need.

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:19.629
Absolutely.

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:20.460
That's the way to do it.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:25.260
And I think it's a challenge because
as you said, there are so many topics.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:28.019
So a lot of students will ask me,
well, what topics do I prepare?

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:28.780
I mean, there's hundreds.

00:09:28.829 --> 00:09:29.569
It could be anything.

00:09:29.619 --> 00:09:31.530
And again, I encourage
them to think about it.

00:09:31.539 --> 00:09:34.589
If you're developing your speaking
skills, whatever you study,

00:09:35.084 --> 00:09:38.814
You'll improve your fluency, your
grammar, and your pronunciation.

00:09:39.344 --> 00:09:42.124
So actually, all you're
left with is vocabulary.

00:09:42.415 --> 00:09:45.084
That's where the different
topics comes into play.

00:09:45.454 --> 00:09:47.984
So you pick and choose your topics.

00:09:48.014 --> 00:09:50.184
You don't need to know
them in depth, right?

00:09:50.184 --> 00:09:54.124
If you're talking about nuclear
power, you don't need to be an expert.

00:09:54.324 --> 00:09:55.884
You don't need to know
that level of depth.

00:09:56.094 --> 00:09:57.564
Just the kind of...

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.659
thing that you might hear on the news.

00:09:59.709 --> 00:10:03.159
So, you know, watching your news
programs, the topics that come up there

00:10:03.199 --> 00:10:07.089
can be useful, and they'll give you that
basic vocabulary you need to be able

00:10:07.089 --> 00:10:09.269
to talk about it, at least in a test.

00:10:09.359 --> 00:10:13.989
Now, I remember in Japan, if I go
and browse the language section of

00:10:13.989 --> 00:10:18.364
a bookstore, there are All kinds
of books with TOEFL vocabulary,

00:10:18.395 --> 00:10:22.155
IELTS vocabulary, and these books
are full of nothing but words.

00:10:22.204 --> 00:10:25.044
How effective are those as a way
of preparing for these exams?

00:10:25.114 --> 00:10:29.274
Oh, very, very ineffective and
misleading, I think, really, to be

00:10:29.274 --> 00:10:32.274
honest, because they're encouraging
people to think in numbers, right?

00:10:32.284 --> 00:10:33.834
So, I need 3000 words.

00:10:33.915 --> 00:10:35.764
Oh, look, here's a PDF with 3000 words.

00:10:35.784 --> 00:10:36.574
I'll just learn them.

00:10:36.804 --> 00:10:40.824
And then, of course, they can't because
they've not learned the context.

00:10:41.149 --> 00:10:44.270
They haven't got the input
that you talk a lot about.

00:10:44.319 --> 00:10:45.270
They're not getting the context.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.389
They're not really understanding
how they can use the word.

00:10:48.409 --> 00:10:49.459
So it never becomes active.

00:10:49.879 --> 00:10:52.310
I think it gives the
element of familiarity.

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:57.020
So going back to a lot of people at school
have learned school lists, lists of words.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:59.639
So there's an element of, I
know what to do with this.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:04.079
Whereas if you put texts in front of
them, they're not as sure quite what

00:11:04.109 --> 00:11:06.170
to do with them for many students.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:07.460
So they need extra support.

00:11:08.115 --> 00:11:12.545
So, I don't know, as a minimum,
as a reference, maybe, maybe,

00:11:12.545 --> 00:11:14.295
but to learn vocabulary, no,

00:11:14.514 --> 00:11:15.014
probably not.

00:11:15.115 --> 00:11:18.244
It's interesting in my sort of
research on how the brain learns,

00:11:18.655 --> 00:11:23.094
it turns out that, uh, familiarity
with lots of contexts, sort of a top

00:11:23.094 --> 00:11:27.254
down approach to learning, it becomes
at a certain point, particularly

00:11:27.254 --> 00:11:31.544
with low frequency vocabulary, more
important than frequency of exposure.

00:11:31.864 --> 00:11:34.484
Now, you would think that being
exposed frequently to sort of

00:11:34.484 --> 00:11:38.395
individual vocabulary items would
be the most important thing, but

00:11:38.395 --> 00:11:41.654
apparently no, because we need
this association with other things.

00:11:41.655 --> 00:11:43.224
That's how the brain retains things.

00:11:43.395 --> 00:11:45.074
It's got to associate it
with some other things.

00:11:45.344 --> 00:11:46.584
So yeah, that's interesting.

00:11:46.584 --> 00:11:49.204
I always looked at these
books, 5000 words for TOEFL.

00:11:49.395 --> 00:11:51.425
How are you going to learn those?

00:11:51.435 --> 00:11:53.254
You think you're learning,
but you're not learning.

00:11:53.274 --> 00:11:55.435
So I think we'll close on that.

00:11:55.435 --> 00:11:58.194
So basically these tests are important.

00:11:58.204 --> 00:12:02.099
We know they're important for academic
uh, you know, getting into university,

00:12:02.109 --> 00:12:07.720
immigration, workforce, and basically
you need to upgrade your overall

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.960
language skills through practicing
all of the four language skills.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:14.790
And then there are people who are
very knowledgeable, like yourself,

00:12:14.869 --> 00:12:19.369
about the specifics of the test
and the things that the test taker

00:12:19.369 --> 00:12:22.490
needs to know about the test in
order to do that little bit better.

